climate change affects everyone but how  do we get everyone to work together to  solve it at the end of the month the  focus will be here in Paris as well  leaders meet to try to agree a plan of  action but in a world filled with  competing interests reaching an  agreement is no easy task let's look at  some of the key players first the big  emitters EU countries relied on fossil  fuels to industrialize and now they're  moving away from heavy industry and  they're encouraging other countries to  lower their emissions too but it's not  that simple rapidly developing countries  like China and India rely on fossil  fuels for economic growth they don't see  why they should have to curb emissions  while others like the EU got to use  fossil fuels in the past then there are  countries like the u.s. they're worried  that if they curb their emissions they'd  lose industry to places like China and  India between them these countries emit  over half the world's carbon dioxide but  what about the countries which will be  heavily affected by global warming  without being able to do much to stop it  places like the Maldives and the  Marshall Islands which risk getting  eaten up by sea level rise in the past  finding a deal that everyone would agree  to seemed impossible after years of  trying to divide up responsibilities in  Copenhagen in 2009 it finally became  clear that no one plan would work for  everyone  so the Paris talks are taking a  different approach  pledges each country independently  pledges how it intends to lower its  emissions these pledges are called  intended nationally determined  contributions this seems to be working  well so far 158 countries have submitted  their pledges for example China have  pledged to peak their co2 emissions by  2030 and the European Union have pledged  to reduce their greenhouse gases by 40%  by the same year but the pledges don't  add up combining them allows you to  estimate how hot the globe will get if  everyone meets their target as it stands  we're facing a world two point seven  degrees warmer than before the  Industrial Revolution still well above  the 2 degree threshold agreed by the  international community  what's more the pledges themselves only  the start of the Paris negotiations will  the deal be legally binding how do we  make sure countries stick to their  pledges and then there's the matter of  money many pledges from developing  countries rely on support from wealthier  states developed countries have  committed to providing a hundred billion  dollars a year from 2020 onwards mostly  through a Green Climate Fund but it's  still not entirely clear where all this  money would come from and which  countries would receive it the task  faced by decision-makers in Paris is  huge and the states can be much higher  what will the deal look like can a deal  even be reached it won't be long before  we know the answers to those questions
 

